<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Stall watchdog for ONE unattended agent session on macOS: a LaunchDaemon that
  runs as the agent's user. The macOS form of agent-stall-watchdog.service.example,
  whose header says what the watchdog is for and why it is a separate service.
  Placeholders:

    <agent>           the watched session's agent name
    <user>            the user that owns the session and its tmux socket
    <checkout>        THAT agent's own project checkout, the working directory only
    <fleet>           the host fleet checkout, which runs watchdog.py
    <agents-root>     the directory holding every agent home
    <state dir>       state.dir from the config, created (mode 0700, owned by
                      <user>) before loading; the log is written there
    <tmux-dir>        the session's socket directory, tmux.tmpdir in the config:
                      <agents-root>/run/claude-tmux-<agent> on macOS
    <launchd-prefix>  host.json launchd_prefix (default com.agent-fleet)
    <python>          the interpreter every fleet job runs, agent-daemons.py's job
                      table's python: FLEET_PYTHON, else host.json's python, else
                      Homebrew's /opt/homebrew/bin/python3
    <path>            the job's PATH: the directory of the tmux the session runs,
                      then the directory of <python>, then /usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin

  installation/render-plist.py fills every placeholder, as the user the job runs
  as, and refuses to render when no tmux is on that user's PATH or when tmux or
  the interpreter does not run under the PATH it would write. Render with it
  rather than by hand: <path> is a fact about the Mac it runs on, and a PATH that
  reaches no tmux is a job that runs, captures nothing and looks healthy.

  A placeholder is an XML tag to the parser, so a file with one left in does not
  load: `plutil -lint` the result. No double hyphen may appear in this comment:
  XML ends a comment there.

  THE PROTECTED FORM, AND THE ONLY ONE check REPORTS AS INSTALLED. A root-owned
  LaunchDaemon with UserName runs as the agent's user, so the agent can kill the
  process (KeepAlive starts it again within ThrottleInterval) but cannot bootout,
  disable or edit the job: all three need root against the system domain. One
  sudo line installs it, after rendering this file to <rendered>:

    sudo install -m 644 -o root -g wheel <rendered> /Library/LaunchDaemons/<launchd-prefix>.stall-watchdog.<agent>.plist && sudo launchctl bootstrap system /Library/LaunchDaemons/<launchd-prefix>.stall-watchdog.<agent>.plist

  THE CONFIG IS PART OF THE PROTECTION. Where an administrator installs this
  for a separate agents user, STALL_WATCHDOG_CONFIG names a root-owned config,
  /etc/agent-fleet/stall-watchdog/<agent>.json (mode 644), as the systemd form's
  system install does: a config in the agent's own home is one the watched agent
  can edit (stall_secs raised to a day, a pattern that never matches), and a
  watchdog its subject can reconfigure is one it can switch off.

  THE INTERPRETER IS PART OF THE PROTECTION TOO. ProgramArguments' first entry,
  <python> (Homebrew's python3 on the supported layout), is what runs
  watchdog.py, and an agents user that can change it runs its own code as the
  watchdog. Homebrew's files belong to the
  account that installed it, so on a Mac with a separate administrator Homebrew
  is the administrator's (install-agent-fleet's bootstrap-target.sh installs it
  that way), and install-agent-fleet's verify-privileges.py fails an
  interpreter the agents user can change, as it fails the script and the plist.

  The same file as a LaunchAgent (without UserName, in the user's
  ~/Library/LaunchAgents, bootstrapped into gui/<uid>) runs too, but the agent
  can bootout, disable or delete it: installation/check reports that form as
  exit 1, UNPROTECTED.

  Not on macOS: the systemd unit's ProtectSystem/ProtectHome/ReadWritePaths and
  NoNewPrivileges. launchd has no namespacing (sandbox-exec is deprecated); the
  process runs as the agent's user, which is the same boundary the session has.

  Deliberately no CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR: this is a supervisor, not an agent (the
  systemd example says why). PATH reaches tmux and <python>; launchd's own PATH
  reaches neither on a Mac. KeepAlive restarts every exit, including the 75 a
  failed self re-exec ends with (a Homebrew upgrade can remove the Cellar path
  the interpreter ran from): the restart runs ProgramArguments' interpreter.

  agent-session.test.py (systemd/) fills every placeholder of this file and
  parses it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string><launchd-prefix>.stall-watchdog.<agent></string>
  <key>UserName</key>
  <string><user></string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string><python></string>
    <string><fleet>/.claude/skills/stall-watchdog/watchdog.py</string>
  </array>
  <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
  <string><checkout></string>
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>TMUX_TMPDIR</key>
    <string><tmux-dir></string>
    <key>STALL_WATCHDOG_CONFIG</key>
    <string><agents-root>/<agent>/config/stall-watchdog.json</string>
    <key>PATH</key>
    <string><path></string>
  </dict>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string><state dir>/watchdog.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string><state dir>/watchdog.log</string>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <true/>
  <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
  <integer>10</integer>
  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Background</string>
</dict>
</plist>
